hi everyone my name is april zang and  today i will be talking about my senior  research project  fellowship through the tea bioinfo  organization  so my project is called predicting coven  19 patient severity with machine  learning  so here is a brief overview of the  topics that i will be covering today  i'll touch on what inspired my project  to methodologies  and then finally wrap up with the  results of my project and with that  let's jump  right in  so last march our world changed in ways  that i couldn't even imagine  for the masks we're wearing right now to  curfews lockdowns practically everyone  has been affected by coven 19  in some way shape or form there is about  3.24 million deaths  in 155 million cases and these two  numbers are constantly growing  last summer my mom actually contracted  coven 19  but fortunately she only had mild  symptoms and recovered quickly  of course i was incredibly grateful but  extremely curious as to what exactly  distinguished my mom  from the millions of hospitalized  patients that were struggling  for each breath that i saw on tv so it  was this experience that  really prompted me to pursue this topic  as a senior research  project  okay so these probably look very  familiar to you guys  right everybody sitting here um probably  you know has done temperature checks or  been asked if they've experienced these  symptoms but  what differentiates these common  symptoms of mild cases  versus life-threatening lung injury that  many severe cova-19 patients face  so again here you can see sars-kova ii  has a few stages depending on the  disease severity  most about 80 percent of patients stay  within the mild to moderate illness  which you can see is that light blue  curve  whereas 20 of patients progress onto  severe illness this is where  ventilators are required or  hospitalization and even  acute respiratory distress syndrome  so again here you can see the end of  this diagram is a little bit complicated  but if we just focus  on the these little pink alveoli so the  alveoli  are the gas exchange sacs of the lungs  here you can see the difference between  healthy infected moderate and severe so  there is a buildup of fluid within  both the moderate and severe cases and  this is what  causes the gas exchange to become more  difficult hence  shortness of breath  okay so now that we know what the virus  causes between mild and severe cases  let's look at host immune response host  immune response is really important to  look at because  how the immune response reacts how the  immune system reacts will  ultimately determine disease severity  and there are of course many factors  that contribute to a patient's reaction  so in severe patients typically a  cytokine storm is common  this causes a widespread and detrimental  impact on multiple organs  loss of severe patients are also  characterized by a hyper inflammation  signature  high levels of neutrophils which are a  type of white blood cell that releases  cytokines which are responsible for the  inflammatory response  okay so having established that my  project objective  is to identify and investigate what  genes  that are responsible for the different  disease severities  so put simply i want to explore the  correlations between gene  expression and disease severity whether  that's a mild or severe covalent  cova 19 disease case and i did this via  machine learning  so the type of data that i chose to  analyze is rna sequencing data so using  rna-seq we can identify what proteins  are being transcribed  which tells us which genes are being  expressed upregulated or down regulated  in using this information i can  investigate the relationships between  gene expression and disease severity  so the output of rna sequencing is a  gene expression table  the data i downloaded from online was a  gigantic  gene expression table with over 19 000  rows  um jeans which were included  and then here you can see this image  describes the basic rna-seq workflow  and for my project i've worked mostly  with the bioinformatics  portion  okay so i obtained my data from  ncbi's publicly accessible database  one of my data selection criteria was to  have a data set with more than 100  samples  this is really important for machine  learning mostly because the algorithm  has more examples to build a  model off of which will improve the  model accuracy  and the data that i ended up choosing  was in transcripts per million and was  derived from  um tissue origin of leukocytes which is  just  the overarching term for all white blood  cells  here you can see this is an image of  some of some of the possible data sets  that i was investigating and i organized  them by  number of samples and other clinical  data that the  researchers may have provided for  example age sex etc  okay so after the data is collected it's  really important to normalize it  as many machine learning algorithms  assume a normally distributed data  so to do this i performed quantile  normalization with a threshold of five  i also performed a logarithmic  transformation  and eliminated any low expression data  which i did via python  so here this is just um a code block of  some python that i wrote this was for  transposing data files  so i actually originally tried to do  this with excel but the program  could not handle a table with over 19  000 rows so i ended up having to do that  in python  and then i also did eliminate any low  expression  data for example any genes that have  zero expression and over half the  samples those genes were eliminated  okay so the next step is the machine  learning portion which is kind of the  bulkier part of the project  so before i jump into this what is  machine learning  machine learning is a type of ai that  takes input data  and investigates the different patterns  between groups within a training data  set  and then we'll apply what is learned to  a test data set  this type of machine learning is known  as supervised machine learning  so this diagram is actually really  deceptive because there are many  intricate parts to machine learning for  instance optimizing your parameters  and before doing this project i had  never heard of these algorithms before  right  random forest svm and naive bayes so  with no previous experience this was one  of the challenges that i had to  overcome throughout this project and  then i  accessed all of these pipelines all  these machine learning algorithms via  the t  bioinfo platform where they have it all  nicely set up  okay so the final step of my project  is to put what i found in a biological  context  so using david which is an annotation  tool  i was able to functionally annotate the  14 genes that my algorithm  that my algorithm had selected and  i'll talk a little bit about how i got  these 14 genes later  i also conducted a thorough literature  review investigating the role  of a few of these 14 genes in kova 19  based on previous findings  okay so here is a table of the different  machine learning algorithm  trials that i ran so you can see they're  grouped by mean decreased  accuracy cutoff abbreviated as mda  the training model and then the test  data classification  training model and test data  classification accuracy excuse me  so mean decreased accuracy basically  represents how important a gene  is to accurately um classifying a set of  data  so for instance if i were to take out a  gene  that had a higher positive mean decrease  accuracy for instance  3.7 then that  the model accuracy would decrease  by that number 3.7 percent  whereas if i were to remove any data  that had  negative mean decrease accuracies that  would improve the classification  accuracy of the  algorithm and so i focused on  this 1.7 mean decreased accuracy cut off  with the  svm algorithm because this trial  had a reasonable balance between the  training model accuracy  which was 95.12 and also minimum number  of features  and usually with machine learning we  want to identify  the minimum number of gene features that  can still accurately classify between  groups in this case mild versus severe  so this is why i  ended up using this trial run  so out of the genes that i'd selected i  investigated  two of them and found them to be linked  in dizzy severity and i'll go more in  depth  for a few of these genes in the next  slides as well but here you can see this  is just a brief snapshot of all the  14 genes that i found  okay so the first gene i investigated  was ccr5 which is a chemokine receptor  protein it's located on the surface of  white blood cells  so previous findings have shown that the  ccr5 expression is significantly  upregulated in sars cova 19  infected cells so if we were to put it  the opposite way that means if we  inhibited this  gene expression in severe cova-19  patients  this would help to reduce inflammatory  cytokines  and decrease the sars covalent to rna in  plasma  so in simple terms identifying this gene  and its role in aiding cova-19 infection  this allows us to intervene and possibly  inhibit ccr5 expression and help  control disease severity so this could  be  a possible therapeutic target to help  treat severe infections  okay so the next gene that i looked at  was h6pd  which is a enzyme that's really  important to reducing  oxidative stress and preventing cell  destruction  so past findings have shown that this  g6pd deficiency  affects about 10 percent of  african-american males so  again in simple terms having a g6pd  enzyme deficiency  may put african-american populations at  a higher risk for developing severe  symptoms due to cover 19. and of course  this may be a plausible explanation  as to why african american populations  seem to be harder hit by cover 19  with higher death rates three to six  times higher compared to other races  so you're probably wondering why is this  research important  this is useful because in the future we  can do a simple  patient blood draw and then run an  rna-seq analysis to see what genes are  upregulated or down regulated and then  for instance some of the genes i  discussed before could possibly serve as  predictors for severe or mild covalent t  and disease severity  so in turn this can really help doctors  know what to expect  and more effectively treat patients in  the future  and then with that i'd like to give a  big thank you to  my on-site advisors dr mr  chione ms sternfield and then my  off-site advisor dr corp  for their invaluable mentorship and  guidance throughout  my research project
 

